# Editor swap files
*.kate-swp

# Scratch staging (agent work product; the durable artifacts are in
# dotfiles-ai/dsh and the plan/checklist files, not here)
scratch/

# Build-only output that must NOT ship: rebuilt locally via
# `pnpm build` / the `prepare` script. node_modules/ and coverage/ stay ignored.
node_modules/
coverage/

# The bundle artifacts below ARE committed on purpose. aidos is installed
# from git (github:), and the package manager blocks git-hosted install-time
# builds until allowBuilds is configured, so lib/client.js,
# dist/host/aidos-plugin.js and presets/aidos/aidos-tools.js must ship from
# the repo instead of being generated at install time.
#
# THE RULE (#148): a commit that touches src/ ships its rebuild. Run
# `node build.mjs` and commit the artifacts it writes, in the same commit as
# the source change. These files are not by-products — they are the product,
# and src/ is only their input.
#
# This is enforced, not remembered, at TWO points that fail independently:
#
#   1. A PRE-COMMIT HOOK refuses the commit. build.mjs writes it, and
#      package.json runs build.mjs as `prepare`, so a fresh clone gets the
#      hook from its first `pnpm install` — no clone has to remember, which
#      is the whole point. It judges the INDEX (what you are committing),
#      not the working tree, so an unstaged rebuild cannot talk it out of a
#      refusal. Measured at ~100ms for the full check and ~50ms when the
#      commit touches nothing the bundles are built from.
#
#      Deliberately committing source WITHOUT its rebuild (splitting a large
#      change, say)? Two documented ways out, both named in the refusal:
#          AIDOS_SKIP_BUNDLE_HOOK=1 git commit ...   # this check only
#          git commit --no-verify                    # every hook
#
#   2. THE SUITE still checks it. tests/u148-bundle-freshness.test.ts fails
#      the normal run when the two sides disagree in either direction, and
#      tests/u148-precommit-hook.test.ts drives the hook itself. This is the
#      belt to the hook's braces: it catches a clone that never installed,
#      and every use of the bypasses above.
#
# Both exist because the rule WAS only remembered, and the shipped client
# bundle silently fell twelve commits behind src/ — four tickets' worth of
# work absent from the artifact consumers install, while every test stayed
# green. The first fix caught it only on the next suite run, i.e. after the
# bad commit already existed; the hook is the correction to that.

